1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * FT 1865: ug Tjenestekarl: ØT: Kihle
2 CONT * FT 1875: Husfader Gaardbr. og Selveier: ØT: OPPEGAARDEN øvre
2 CONT * Utvandret 1878 #77 [https://sites.google.com/site/totenemigrant/Toten-emigrant-1846/1876-1880 Link]<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT "Henrik Oppegard, another old pioneer settler of Barnes County, has been called to his eternal reward. The summons came at 5 o'clock Sunday evening at his home on the Schindeldecker farm southwest of town. Goiter and other complic
2 CONC ations was the cause of death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deceased was born in Norway in July 1843 and was nearly 70 years of age. He came to Barnes County and homesteaded south of Valley City about 35 years ago. Some twenty years ago he came to Litchville and has been a resident of this
2 CONC  city and vicinity ever since.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by an aged wife in poor health and six children: Mrs. J. Thoreson, Route 3, Mrs. Mads Larson of Route 1, Mrs. Minnie Swanson of Valley City, Mrs. N. R. Nelson, Moorhead, Miss Gussie Oppegard of Fessenden and Haagen,
2 CONC  living at the old home.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral was held from the home at 2 p.m. Wednesday, Rev. Eidnes officiating. The pall bearers were: J.B. Christensen, Esten Aus, Hans Olson, John Jacobson, John Bruschwein and Charles Bruschwein. Interment was made in the Norw
2 CONC egian cemetery at Griswold.
2 CONT
2 CONT All the children were present at the funeral as well as two brothers, John Billet of Valley City and Haagen Billet of Hastings.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sorrowing family have the sympathy of all in their sorrow."
2 CONT
2 CONT Bio by: Myrna (Lindstrom) McGregor
