1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * FT 1875: Datter ug Hjelper Faderen: ØT: OPPEGAARDEN øvre
2 CONT * Utvandret 1878 #80 [https://sites.google.com/site/totenemigrant/Toten-emigrant-1846/1876-1880 Link]<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mrs. Mads Larson, born Theoline Oppegaard, first opened her eyes to the light of day in Toten, Norway, on the 6th day of December, 1870. When a child, seven years of age, she came to the United States with her parents, who settle
2 CONC d in Fillmore county, Minn. Four years later, in 1881, the family moved to North Dakota, settling in this community, and here she grew to womanhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 16th, 1889, she was married to Mr. Mads Larson, and they have lived on their farm northeast of town almost ever since. During the past five years, Mrs. Larson had been a sufferer with cancer of the left breast, and thi
2 CONC s dread disease was the cause of her death. The final summons came on Sunday morning, September 8th, at the hour of 9:50 o’clock, at the age of fifty-eight years, nine months and two days.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral was held on Wednesday afternoon, September 11th, with services at the Ringsaker church, north of Hastings, Rev. Fredrick Dahl, pastor of the church, officiated and the remains were laid to rest in the churchyard cemete
2 CONC ry. The pall bearers were: Severt Skolness, Carl Johnson, C. W. Erickson, Ole Fossum, Erick Fossum and Martin Giste.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving are the bereaved husband, three sisters, namely: Mrs. John Thorson, of LItchville township; Mrs. N. R. Nelson, of Moorhead, Minn., and Mrs. Gussie Simenson, of Canada; and one brother, Haaken Oppegaard, of this city. The
2 CONC  sorrowing relatives have the sympathy of the entire community.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Larson was a splendid person, a friendly woman who always had a ready smile and happy word for the wayfarer on the road of life.
2 CONT
2 CONT May she rest in peace."
2 CONT
2 CONT Published in the Litchville Bulletin, Litchville, North Dakota, September 20, 1929.
