1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * FT 1875: Søn ug Hjelper Faderen: ØT: OPPEGAARDEN øvre
2 CONT * Utvandret 1878 #81 [https://sites.google.com/site/totenemigrant/Toten-emigrant-1846/1876-1880 Link]<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT "Funeral services were held on Friday afternoon for Haagen Oppegaard from the First Lutheran Church with Rev. E. Kristensen in charge. Burial will be made in the Litchville cemetery in the spring.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pallbearers were: Carl Fredrickson, T. I. Strinden, Fred Songberg, Harry Peterson, Louis Sonsthagen and K. K. Wangrud. Oliver's Chapel was in charge of funeral arrangements.
2 CONT
2 CONT Haagen Oppegaard was born in East Toten, Norway, March 1873. He came with his folks from Norway to Fillmore country, Minnesota in 1879. The family came to Valley City in 1880, and his parents homesteaded near the Ringsaker Luthera
2 CONC n Church. Later they moved to what was then known as the Fred Johnson farm (now occupied by the Leo Schall family).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Oppegaard worked on farms near Litchville in the early days, working for pioneer Kelley, P. N. Peterson, R. H. Schindeldecker and others. Later he rented land from Schindeldecker and continued to farm until both of his parents
2 CONC  had passed on. In 1915 he sold his farming equipment and moved into Litchille where he has lived for the past 38 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He leaves three sisters: Mrs. Mathilda Thorson, Litchville; Mrs. N. R. Nelson, Fargo; and Mrs. C. A. Simenson, Blue Ridge, Alberta, Canada (who was unable to attend the funeral.)
2 CONT
2 CONT His parents and two sisters preceded him in
2 CONT death. The sisters were: Mrs. Minnie Swanson who died in 1918 and Mrs. Lena Larson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relatives from a distance attending the funeral were: Mrs N. R. Nelson, Fargo; Me. and Mrs. Henry Haakenson, Fargo; Mrs. G. Berntson, Hastings; Mrs. Arthur Monson, West Fargo; Mr. and Mrs. Richard Eilts and family, Mrs. Richard Tr
2 CONC iepke and son of Marion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blessed be his memory.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the service, members of Circle No. 1 served lunch in the church parlors."
2 CONT
2 CONT Bio by: Myrna (Lindstrom) McGregor
